WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:13.760
Graphs are useful tools for data exploration.&nbsp;
For example, bar graphs and pie charts are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.680
good for categorical data; histograms, box&nbsp;
plots and time plots for connotative data;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.200
and, scatter plot to examine the relationship&nbsp;
between a pair of quantitative variables.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:34.320
Bar graph is also called a bar chart. In the&nbsp;
basic version of this graph, each bar represent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.800
category and the height of the bar represents&nbsp;
the count or percentage of each category.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.160
The bar graph on this slide shows the search&nbsp;
engine market share for different search engines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.840
It shows that Google has the dominant market&nbsp;
share in search engine market followed by Yahoo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:56.880
and Bing. There are many different variants of bar&nbsp;
graphs. In a Pareto chart, categories are ranked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.800
from the most popular to the least popular. The&nbsp;
bar graph on this previous slide is an example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.240
Pareto charts make it easier to spot top&nbsp;
categories, especially when the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:15.280
categories are large. A bar graph can be shown&nbsp;
vertically or horizontally. Although horizontal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.840
bar graphs are more common, vertical bar&nbsp;
graphs can sometimes be useful as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.080
In stacked bar graphs, bars are stacked together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.440
making it easier to put multiple categories on the&nbsp;
same graph or show the same category over time.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:40.080
Pie chart uses pie slices to represent the&nbsp;
percentage share of different categories. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.960
is similar to a bar graph, but emphasizes&nbsp;
each category as a part of one whole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.800
In other words when we used pie chart we implicit&nbsp;
and assumed that the category is exhaustive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.440
This is different from a bar&nbsp;
graph. The pie chart on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.200
slide shows the same information as&nbsp;
the bar graph in the previous slide.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.640
Next, we will discuss a few basic&nbsp;
graph to chart quantitative data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.040
A histogram is a summary plot&nbsp;
for a single numerical variable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.640
It shows a distribution of the data giving some&nbsp;
sense on basic characteristics of the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:22.240
Including center variability, skewness, modality,&nbsp;
outliers, and possibly other patterns. As an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.840
example, this graph shows a histogram of&nbsp;
exam scores. We can clearly see that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:34.240
distribution is bimodal, with 1 mode between&nbsp;
65 and 70, and the other 1 between 80 and 85.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:41.200
The distribution is centered around 70 and is&nbsp;
highly variable with a range between 50 and 95.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.000
The distribution is lightly skewed to&nbsp;
the right, with no visible outliers.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.480
For small data sets, histograms can be&nbsp;
misleading. A small changes in your data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:00.000
can alter the overall pattern. For large&nbsp;
data sets, histograms really work well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.200
Even though histograms can only show&nbsp;
one variable at a time, side by side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:11.360
histograms can be used to compare distribution of&nbsp;
multiple variables. When constructing histograms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:17.600
it is important to choose appropriate beam widths,&nbsp;
or the widths of the individual bars. If small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:23.520
pinwheels is used, the graph can show too&nbsp;
much detail, and be hard to read. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.000
using large pinwheels can lead to misleading&nbsp;
patterns and give the wrong real impression.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:37.440
A second plot to show quantitative data is box&nbsp;
plot. The plot shows a five number summary of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:43.440
single variable. The five summary statistics are&nbsp;
shown with solid horizontal lines in the graph.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:49.920
The five lines from bottom to top represent,&nbsp;
respectively, the minimum, first quartile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:56.080
second quartile or the median, third quartile,&nbsp;
and the maximum. Note that in calculating those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:02.160
statistics, the outliers, which are represented&nbsp;
by the dots in the graph, are removed. The big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:08.080
box in the plot which is between first and third&nbsp;
quartile contains half of the data. The inside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:14.240
the box represents the median with 50% of the&nbsp;
data on each side. The box plot effectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:20.480
divides the data into four quarters, and clearly&nbsp;
marks their boundaries. However, it is difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:27.840
to tell the distribution shape. Also, different&nbsp;
tools have different implementation of the plot.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:03.600
Side by side box plots are very effective in&nbsp;
showing differences in a quantitative variable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.960
across factor levels. The graph shows side&nbsp;
by side the lease prices of real estate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:14.880
properties with and without a parking garage. It&nbsp;
is difficult to establish distinguishing shapes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.880
the lease prices. The graph shows unequally that&nbsp;
properties with parking garage are more expensive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.480
In addition, we can also easily grab a few&nbsp;
useful summary statistics from the graphs.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.960
Time plot can be used when there is&nbsp;
a meaningful sequence such as time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:39.280
This graph shows a Google trend for the search&nbsp;
term gains since 2004, the graph shows a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:46.080
declining trend from 2004 to 2016. In addition,&nbsp;
there is a clear early pattern in the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:52.000
The number of searches peaks in January&nbsp;
and generally bottoms out in the Summer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.440
In the time plot, time should always go&nbsp;
on the horizontal axis. We describe time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:02.080
series by looking for an overall pattern and&nbsp;
for striking deviations from that pattern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:10.000
A trend is a rise or fall that proceeds over&nbsp;
time. These spikes spot irregularities. A pattern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.240
that repeats itself at regular intervals&nbsp;
at a time is called seasonal variation.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:19.840
A scatter plot shows the relationship&nbsp;
between two unrelated variables.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:25.440
This scatter plot shows the relationship&nbsp;
between Old Faithful geyser eruption duration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.480
and we can hide between eruptions. They're&nbsp;
appears to be a nearly linear relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:36.240
between the two as illustrated in the graph. In&nbsp;
addition, there are two linear groups of data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:42.000
Data points in the bottom left have shorter&nbsp;
eruption durations, and short waiting times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:48.160
while the data points on the top right have&nbsp;
long erupting durations and long waiting times.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:53.120
Since erupting time and waiting times between&nbsp;
eruptions tend to move together, the relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:59.680
appears to be positive. Typically, the exploratory&nbsp;
or independent variable is plotted on the x-axis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:06.560
And response or dependent variable is plotted&nbsp;
on the y-axis. Each pair of data appear as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:13.920
a point in plot. A relationship can be either&nbsp;
positive or negative. In a positive association,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.480
high values of one variable tend to occur&nbsp;
together with high values of the other variable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.720
This is the case for Old Faithful data&nbsp;
shown before. In a negative association,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:29.840
high values of one variable tend to occur&nbsp;
together with low values of the other variable.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:38.720
The relationship between two variables may not be&nbsp;
linear. The Old Faithful for data shows a nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:44.400
linear relationship. As we mentioned before,&nbsp;
this relationship appears to be positive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:50.480
there are so many other possibilities as shown&nbsp;
here. The relationship can be linear or nonlinear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:56.240
Relationship can take many different&nbsp;
forms. It can also be the case that no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:01.040
visible pattern appears in the scatter plot, in&nbsp;
which case we say that there is no relationship.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:09.440
Another important idea is strength of&nbsp;
relationship. The strength of a relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:13.840
between the two variables can be seen by how&nbsp;
much variation or scatter there is around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:19.200
main form. With a strong relationship, you can&nbsp;
get a pretty good estimate of why if you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:25.840
the value of x. With a weak relationship, for any&nbsp;
x value, you might get a wide range of y values.

